<h2 id='who-its-for'>🎯 Who AWeber Is (and Isn't) For</h2>

<p><strong>AWeber works well for:</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>Independent tour operators and boutique travel agencies with lists under 25,000 subscribers</li>
  <li>Agencies that want reliable deliverability without a complex setup</li>
  <li>Teams without a dedicated email marketing specialist — the learning curve is shallow</li>
  <li>Operators sending regular destination newsletters, seasonal promotions, and post-booking sequences</li>
  <li>Agencies that also want to build landing pages without paying for a separate tool</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>AWeber is a poor fit for:</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>High-volume operators running complex, multi-branch lifecycle automations</li>
  <li>Agencies that need deep native integrations with booking platforms like FareHarbor or Rezdy</li>
  <li>Operators who want built-in CRM functionality alongside email marketing</li>
  <li>Enterprise travel companies managing multiple brands under one account (the Unlimited plan is priced accordingly)</li>
</ul>

<p>If you're in that second camp, we'd point you toward our <a href='https://traveloperator.io/reviews/activecampaign-review-travel-agencies'>ActiveCampaign review for travel agencies</a>, which handles advanced automation considerably better, though at a higher price point and steeper learning curve.</p>

